One session per *thread*, not per process: the frozen clock.

timestamp.cc caches the frozen clock in `millis_cache`. Each session freezes the
clock at the top of its own loop, so with two sessions running, each one reads
whatever instant the other froze. mosh's RTT and RTO estimates and its timestamp
echoes are all computed from that value, so the symptom is not a visibly wrong
clock — it is a transport that mis-measures the network and stops retransmitting
correctly.

Found by runtime validation against a live mosh-server, not by any test: with
one session the static is correct, and Sloop only runs two when the user opens a
second tab.

Same fix and same reasoning as 0001-thread-local-compressor.patch.
Applies to mosh-1.4.0.

--- a/src/util/timestamp.cc
+++ b/src/util/timestamp.cc
@@ -58,7 +58,7 @@
 #define CLOCKTYPE CLOCK_MONOTONIC
 #endif
 
-static uint64_t millis_cache = -1;
+static thread_local uint64_t millis_cache = -1;
 
 uint64_t frozen_timestamp( void )
 {
